<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">Years in prison for A and B, depending on both choices.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto border-2 border-ink"><table class="w-full text-base">
<thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-ink"><th class="p-3 text-left">A’s choice</th><th class="p-3 text-center">B stays silent</th><th class="p-3 text-center">B testifies</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr class="border-b border-ink"><th class="p-3 text-left">A stays silent</th><td class="p-3 text-center">A: 1 · B: 1</td><td class="p-3 text-center">A: 3 · B: 0</td></tr><tr><th class="p-3 text-left">A testifies</th><td class="p-3 text-center">A: 0 · B: 3</td><td class="p-3 text-center">A: 2 · B: 2</td></tr></tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base"><div class="border-l-4 border-[#387a91] pl-3"><strong>If B stays silent</strong><p>A gets 0 years by testifying instead of 1.</p></div><div class="border-l-4 border-[#bd4b37] pl-3"><strong>If B testifies</strong><p>A gets 2 years by testifying instead of 3.</p></div></div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The arithmetic gives each player the same reason to testify against a fixed choice by the other. Yet if both testify, they each get 2 years instead of the 1 year each would get by staying silent. Whether that argument settles rational choice is the dispute.</figcaption></figure>